# Project: Jest JavaScript Unit Test Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Jest as the primary test runner for JavaScript/TypeScript projects
- Organize tests in __tests__ directories or co-locate with source files using .test.js/.spec.js extensions
- Use TypeScript with ts-jest or Babel transform for type-safe test implementations
- Configure Jest via jest.config.js or jest.config.ts at the project root

## Coding Standards
- Follow JavaScript/TypeScript best practices and consistent naming conventions
- Use meaningful describe and test/it block names that read as plain English
- Keep tests short and focused on a single behavior
- Limit describe block nesting to 3 levels maximum

## Test Organization
- Group related tests using describe blocks
- Use it/test functions for individual test cases
- Implement beforeEach/afterEach and beforeAll/afterAll for setup and teardown
- Separate unit, integration, and component tests into distinct directories

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern for clear test structure
- Write focused, single-responsibility tests with one logical assertion per test
- Avoid test interdependencies and shared mutable state between tests
- Use jest.fn() for creating mock functions instead of manual mocks
- Clear mocks between tests using jest.clearAllMocks() or clearMocks: true in config

## Mocking Strategies
- Use jest.mock() for module-level mocking with automatic hoisting
- Use jest.spyOn() to observe and optionally mock existing methods
- Use jest.useFakeTimers() for testing setTimeout, setInterval, and Date
- Mock HTTP requests with msw (Mock Service Worker) or jest.mock() on axios/fetch
- Use jest.resetModules() when module state must be isolated between tests

## Assertions
- Use expect() with descriptive matchers (toBe, toEqual, toContain, toThrow)
- Implement custom matchers via expect.extend() for domain-specific assertions
- Use toMatchSnapshot() and toMatchInlineSnapshot() for stable output testing
- Prefer toEqual for deep equality checks over toBe for objects and arrays
- Use expect.objectContaining() and expect.arrayContaining() for partial matches

## Coverage
- Configure coverage with --coverage flag or collectCoverage: true in config
- Use istanbul (default) as the coverage provider for detailed reports
- Set meaningful coverage thresholds for branches, functions, lines, and statements
- Use coveragePathIgnorePatterns to exclude generated or non-testable files
- Generate lcov reports for CI integration with coverage services

## Performance
- Use --runInBand for debugging; rely on default worker pool for CI speed
- Implement test.concurrent for independent async tests when needed
- Use --testPathPattern or --testNamePattern to run targeted subsets during development
- Configure maxWorkers appropriately for CI environments to avoid resource contention

## Component Testing
- Integrate jest-environment-jsdom for DOM-based component tests
- Use Testing Library (@testing-library/react, /vue, /svelte) for user-centric assertions
- Mock child components only when isolating the unit under test is critical
- Use screen.getByRole, getByLabelText, and getByText over implementation-detail selectors

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions for creating reusable, composable test data
- Define TypeScript interfaces for all test data structures
- Avoid hardcoded magic values; use named constants or fixtures
- Generate dynamic data with @faker-js/faker when realistic variety is needed

## CI/CD Integration
- Use --ci flag in CI environments to disable watch mode and fail on missing snapshots
- Use --reporters=default and jest-junit for JUnit-compatible CI reporting
- Cache node_modules and Jest's transform cache between CI runs for speed
- Upload coverage reports to Codecov, SonarQube, or similar services

## Environment Configuration
- Use jest.config.js testEnvironment to switch between node and jsdom
- Configure moduleNameMapper for path aliases and CSS/asset mocks
- Use setupFilesAfterFramework for global matchers and testing-library setup
- Handle environment variables with dotenv or cross-env in test scripts

## Error Handling
- Test both success and failure paths explicitly
- Assert on specific error messages and types using toThrow(ErrorClass, 'message')
- Use expect(asyncFn()).rejects.toThrow() for async error assertions
- Ensure rejected promises are always awaited to avoid false positives

## Snapshot Testing
- Keep snapshot files in version control alongside test files
- Update snapshots intentionally with --updateSnapshot, not as a habit
- Prefer toMatchInlineSnapshot() for small outputs to keep context local
- Delete obsolete snapshots regularly to avoid maintaining stale baselines

## Documentation
- Document complex test scenarios with brief inline comments explaining intent
- Maintain a test helper and fixture README for shared utilities
- Include examples for custom matchers and mock factories
- Document project-specific module mock strategies and any jest.config quirks

Remember to leverage Jest's rich ecosystem (jest-extended, jest-axe, jest-mock-extended) and keep tests deterministic, isolated, and fast.
